Z||000000||Slammed Fiero||02-01-2001||11:33 AM||||These are froze. I have never changed the rear pads in the 5 years and 40,000kms I have had the car (there still good) but I have replaced the fronts 3 times. Any suggestions to getting them off?? Should I use an impacet on them?? The car is getting new rear calipers anyway so....<P>Also what should I pay for a Blazer master cylinder...<P><BR>Going for 11.25" vented all around with willwoods (4 piston) up front<P><BR>Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||02-01-2001||12:07 PM||||Jonathan,<P>I used a breaker bar on a socket wrench.  Although, you may want to apply some PB blaster or other liquid wrench type product.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||02-01-2001||02:17 PM||||if you have access to a torch, try heating it up. wont matter if you burn up seals, sense your gonna replace the calipers anyway. keep fire extinguisher handy.I try to snap them loose with a jerk instead of slow steady pressure, less chance of bolt breaking.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wannabe||02-01-2001||04:53 PM||||Did you fabricate your own mounting brackets for the Wilwood calipers or where are you getting them? What about the hats for the rotors?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||02-01-2001||06:33 PM||||I would try an impact wrench.  The rapid pulses of torque will break bolts loose alot better than a steady application of torque using a breaker bar.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Slammed Fiero||02-01-2001||07:48 PM||||The rotors are standard 89-94 chrysler LEbaron , there 11.25" vented discs, The rears will fit perfectly but need longer studs. as for the brackets I got some cad drawings off of the internet for the rears , the fronts are going to have to be fabricated , but thats no real big deal. The brackets are 3/8th's " steel. I have a complete parts list and there are 2 versions floating around the internet on the setup , you need 2 have 16" wheels at least, I don't know about using the Wilwoods with 16" wheels though. The original setup uses camaro calipers. I wanted the larger 4 piston Wilwoods for my setupo since the car sees track use and I have plans for 300+hp. I have talked to a fellow named greg Duncan one of the pioneers of the 4.9 caddy swap he uses them on his autocross/ beast , according to him he can outbrake c4 vettes with ease as well he is right on par with 911's. I have driven a Few 911's and the braking is nothing short of heart stopping, 11.8" on a 911 and they weigh about 100lbs more than the average GT. I got kinda frustrated with my stock brakes , not only did they look tiny peaking out through 17" wheels , they overheated at the track even when properly set up.<P>You do need fron hub adaptors the same as you would with any other fiero front brake conversion. I know where there is a set for sale if anyone is interested. There of no real economic sense to me with the canadian dollar being at what it's at.<P>If anyone is interested chrismoore has them as well as a ton of other fiero parts for sale , he also does N* conversions...best I have seen<P>northstar87@excite.com<P>In any case I will keep you all posted as to the conversion , I got a call tonight from the shop that my rotors are all in , The Willwood calipers are $99 each from summit.<BR>I will also have pics comparing the brakes to a C4 vette (87 with Grand sport upgrade) and a 94 911 RS america porsche , I will do a few 60 to zero runs too with the GTEC... Keep in mind I have pretty soft tires , buut you will get the idea.<P> <P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||02-02-2001||03:45 PM||||All of my Fiero mods are for the track, so the Wilwoods and large rotors make perfect sense. Single piston, stock calipers will always go soft on the track. It would be nice to keep away from 17" rims though. Fiero Shop in Toronto also sells billet aluminum hubs for 84-87 Fiero brake conversions. They are not cheap, but the though of those 10+ lb hubs just doesn't appeal to me.<P>How often do you get out to the track? Some friends and I rented the pro track at Shannonville last summer and only one other Fiero owner showed up. There doesn't seem to be too much track interest in the Fiero community.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Slammed Fiero||02-02-2001||05:03 PM||||Got any pics of your car?? what year is it? lat year Ididn't get much of a chance to run the fiero, I ran our vette a few times and the RS. Our club rents shannonville about 5 times a summer , and we have one complete lapping day , lotsa fun....where are you located??<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Wannabe||02-02-2001||10:17 PM||||I was in Mississauga, now I'm in Stoney Creek (20 min from the new track at Cayuga!) My Fiero is hardly what you'd call track ready - it's barely road ready - but I did get it running for the lapping day last summer. That was all I did with it. It's an '87 GT, blue. I will be doing a complete suspension, brakes , exhaust, roll bar and harness as I'll be competing in Solo 1 again this year. '99 was the last year I ran, in an Eagle Talon. Which club are you with?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||02-03-2001||10:17 AM||||St lawrence autoclub out of kingston , Lots of one lap of america guys... We have a regional event at an air force base about 40 min south of shannonville which is lots of fun , we have nightly lapping sessions and solo 2 events on shannonvilees go kart circuit...<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
